  "text": "Allophone: A variant form of a phoneme that differs only in certain phonetic environments.\n\nAnaphora: A word or phrase that refers back to a previously mentioned word or phrase.\n\nApophony: A change in a vowel or consonant sound in one syllable that affects the sound of a following syllable.\n\nArticulatory phonetics: The study of how speech sounds are produced by the vocal tract.\n\nAspirated consonant: A consonant produced with a burst of breath following the articulation.\n\nAssimilation: A process in which one sound becomes more like a neighboring sound.\n\nBilingualism: The ability to speak and understand two languages.\n\nChordal phonation: A type of vocal fold vibration that produces a buzzing or vibrating quality in sounds.\n\nClitic: A word fragment that is grammatically dependent on another word.\n\nContrastive analysis: A method used in language learning to compare two languages to identify differences.\n\nConsonant cluster: A group of consonants that occur together in a word without a vowel between them.\n\nDiphthong: A vowel sound in which the vowel is pronounced with a glide from one vowel to another.\n\nDialect: A regional or social variety of a language differing in vocabulary, pronunciation, and grammar.\n\nDiphthongization: The process by which a monophthong becomes a diphthong.\n\nEjective consonant: A type of consonant produced with a burst of air from the closure of the vocal tract.\n\nEgressive: A direction of airflow out of the body, typical in most speech sounds.\n\nFricative: A consonant sound produced by narrowing the vocal tract so that the air causes friction.\n\nGlottal stop: A stop sound produced by closing the glottis, blocking the airflow.\n\nGrapheme: A single written symbol in a writing system, such as 'a' or 'th'.\n\nIntonation: The pitch pattern of a sentence, used to convey attitude, emotion, or grammatical function.\n\nLabial consonant: A consonant produced with the lips, such as /p/, /b/, or /m/.\n\nLateral consonant: A consonant produced by directing the airstream sideways through the mouth, such as /l/.\n\nLexical stress: The emphasis placed on a particular syllable in a word.\n\nMorpheme: The smallest meaningful unit of language.\n\nNasal consonant: A consonant produced with the soft palate lowered, allowing air to flow through the nose, such as /m/, /n/, or /\u014b/.\n\nObstruent: A type of consonant made with a complete or partial closure, such as stops, fricatives, and affricates.\n\nOversimplification: A common mistake in language learning where complex grammar or vocabulary is simplified excessively.\n\nPhoneme: A unit of sound in a language that distinguishes one word from another.\n\nPhonology: The study of the sound system of a language.\n\nProsody: The pattern of stress, intonation, and rhythm in speech.\n\nProto-language: A reconstructed ancestral language from which a group of related languages is derived.\n\nRegister: A variety of a language used in specific contexts or by particular groups.\n\nSibilant: A type of fricative with a high-frequency sound, such as /s/, /z/, /\u0161/, /\u0436/.\n\nStress: The emphasis on a syllable in a word or phrase.\n\nVowel: A speech sound produced without a significant obstruction in the vocal tract.\n\nVoiceless consonant: A consonant produced without vibration of the vocal cords.\n\nVoiced consonant: A consonant produced with vibration of the vocal cords.\n\nVoice: The vibration of the vocal folds, which is present in voiced sounds.\n\nWord stress: The emphasis on a particular syllable in",
